Notebook[{
Cell["PadicOrder", "ObjectName",ExpressionUUID->"03db3a44-9a92-4aa1-9d25-e8f209fa6ca0"],

Cell["\<\
PadicOrder[r,p] gives the valuation of an exact integer or rational r in \
integer base p>1. PadicOrder[s] uses the base of an exact, lazy, or finite \
p-adic object; PadicOrder[s,p] also accepts an explicit base. Exact zero has \
order Infinity. A finite-precision zero known to O(p^a) has order \
Interval[{a,Infinity}]. Finite snapshots support only their stored base; lazy \
objects can be inspected at another prime base.\
\>", "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"fe50350d-eeba-4f73-9447-27710ff7e843"],

Cell["\<\
Scalar inputs may use any integer base greater than one; the p-adic \
interpretation requires a prime. Exact objects can be inspected at another \
base using their exact rational value.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"f1c20b4e-4012-4696-a142-cad2614778f5"],

Cell["\<\
For finite-precision objects an explicit base must match the stored base. An \
uncertain zero has only a lower bound on its order.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"65c81a8d-1c15-40f6-9ef7-ab669998a8ff"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Needs", "[", "\"\<PadicNumbers`\>\"", 
  "]"}]], "ExampleInitialization",ExpressionUUID->"8831b56e-c8ea-47b9-8627-\
855cf11ada91"],

Cell["Basic Examples", "PrimaryExamplesSection",ExpressionUUID->"e8016096-a979-496e-9222-d5e3f5faaaa9"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

